    "narrative": "The heart of the lineup for Ghost Ride the WHIP centers on the elite matchup for **Corbin Carroll** (85.94), who draws a favorable draw against Mike Paredes. With an optimized batting order and a significant platoon advantage, **Corbin Carroll** is our primary engine for stolen base production today. He is closely followed by **Gabriel Moreno** (69.96) and **Brice Turang** (69.63). **Brice Turang** in particular is a high-value play; while the raw projection is solid, the presence of a slow-delivering veteran like Bryce Elder on the mound provides an extra 4% efficiency boost to his stolen base potential, making him a critical start for our 5x5 objectives. **James Wood** (65.61) also warrants attention; despite a park factor penalty, his elite 0.404 xwOBA and top-of-the-order usage keep him as a must-start over lower-tier options.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Max Muncy** (46.69), who was the first cut from the starting nine. While **Max Muncy** benefits from a strong park factor against Reid Detmers, his projected spot at #8 in the lineup and the general volatility of his platoon splits pushed him just below the threshold required to displace **Kyle Karros** or **Tyler Soderstrom**. Meanwhile, the squad faces a few tactical risks today: the \"Lineup Pending\" status for almost every player adds variance to our scoring. Specifically, **Seiya Suzuki** (47.72) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (47.23) are working against elite SP skill from Dylan Cease and Reid Detmers, respectively. These players are held in the lineup primarily to maintain our game caps, but owners should monitor the official confirmed lineups, as any further demotion in the batting order for **Zach McKinstry** or **Dalton Rushing**\u2014both already dealing with -8% order penalties\u2014could see their efficiency scores drop into the sub-40 danger zone.",
